00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:05.510 align:start position:0%
 
your majesty  the king has spoken of heavy  responsibilities  and this is the inescapable reality a  diary of duties meetings with  dignitaries and like any bereaved son  the planning of a funeral  we now know on sunday the queen's coffin  will leave balmoral for the palace of  hollywood house in edinburgh  on monday the king and queen consort  will travel to westminster hall where  both houses of parliament will express  condolences and the king will make his  reply  the queen's coffin will be taken to  saint giles's cathedral in edinburgh the  king and queen consort and other members  of the family will follow the coffin in  a procession  and for 24 hours the coffin will rest in  the cathedral so the public can pay  their respects  that evening the king and other royals  will mount a vigil  on tuesday the king and queen consort  will visit northern ireland  meanwhile that evening the queen's  coffin arrives in london and will rest  at buckingham palace  the following day on wednesday the  coffin will be taken to the palace of  westminster in a silent procession  followed by the king and royal family  the queen's coffin will arrive here at  westminster hall for the lying in state  five days during which members of the  public can pay their respects in person  there will be echoes of the past this is  the lying in state of king george vi in  1952. back then huge queues formed to  file past the coffin  similar scenes are expected now  on friday the king and queen consort  visit wales as their tour of the nations  continues  and on monday the 19th of september the  state funeral a procession will take the  queen's coffin to westminster abbey  the service will start here at 11  o'clock there'll be a bank holiday to  mark the day and afterwards a committal  service at windsor as the late queen is  finally laid to rest  it will be beautifully put together  bringing together all the agencies of  the state and the armed forces with the  finest drill the best pageantry and the  noblest of ceremonial in westminster  abbey it will be  a great salute to a wonderful queen  history tells us there will be huge  crowds last seen at the funeral of the  queen mother  but perhaps none will match the moment  of the final farewell of her daughter  queen elizabeth ii  laura bundock sky news westminster

